---
// Hand-authored inline SVG: what one change of address costs in each of the
// three systems the post ranks, and what propagates afterwards. Inline for the
// same reasons as ThreeQuestions.astro — see that file's header.
//
// READ THIS BEFORE EDITING EITHER THIS FILE OR THE POST.
//
// The prose states what one address change costs and names the Portuguese and
// US targets from the author's experience. The drawing supplies the complete
// Italian fan-out and makes the shared skeleton visible. It follows the three
// paragraphs it illustrates, so the caption states what the picture adds rather
// than walking the reader through those paragraphs again.
//
// PROVENANCE:
//      Italy    A change of residence entered once in ANPR is notified
//               automatically to Agenzia delle Entrate, INPS, INAIL, ISTAT and
//               the Motorizzazione. Documented by the Ministry's own ANPR
//               portal (anpr.interno.it) and Italy's digital-transition
//               department (innovazione.gov.it). Order matches the post's
//               original sentence; do not reorder or abbreviate.
//      Portugal Finanças for tax, the câmara for residence, AIMA for
//               immigration. This is a sample, not the complete set; the post
//               says "and anyone else" and the caption says these are three of
//               its owners. Do not present them as exhaustive.
//      US       DMV, IRS, Social Security Administration, bank, utility
//               company — the five the post names in the paragraph above.
//
// POST ANCHORS:
//      Italy    "a resident writes the change once to the national population
//                register, the ANPR, and downstream agencies can receive it
//                from there."
//      Portugal "Updating my address still means telling Finanças, the
//                municipality, AIMA, and anyone else separately."
//      US       "My address is written independently at the DMV, the IRS,
//                Social Security, my bank, and my utility company."
//
// THE CAPTION MUST NOT RECAP THE PROSE. It used to, which was right when the
// diagram ran ahead of the paragraphs — it previewed them. Following them, the
// same words landed as an echo one line later ("so this is replication rather
// than a true foreign key" appeared twice on screen). A caption in payoff
// position states what the PICTURE adds: here, the shared skeleton and the one
// slot that differs between the three bands.
//
// PLACEMENT: this diagram sits AFTER the three paragraphs, not before them.
// That is the house pattern (see the-atlas-data-was-the-hard-part.mdx, where
// every diagram follows the argument it confirms). It ran before them until
// Aug 2026, which is why the prose read as a restatement of a picture the
// reader had already seen. Do not move it back above the Italy paragraph.
//
// One paragraph follows the diagram and returns to the immigration system. It
// is a hinge into the political section, not a fourth band; the picture remains
// a comparison of Italy, Portugal and a US citizen.
//
// The three bands deliberately share one skeleton — "you move" on the left,
// targets on the right — so the only thing that varies between them is what
// sits in the middle. Italy has a register there, Portugal has a key pointing
// at a record that does not exist, and the US has nothing at all. Keep that
// skeleton if you edit any band; the comparison is the whole diagram.
//
// The missing canonical record is drawn with .dg-node--out, the same dashed
// "present on the page, absent from the result" treatment RegionGraphNYC uses
// for New Jersey. It is an absence, not a component — do not fill it in.
//
// Deliberately absent: the composite US immigration case. It would be a fourth
// propagation story assembled across several statuses, and CitizenAsymmetry
// already draws that distinction where the post first makes it.
//
// Styles live in global.css (.post-diagram, .dg-*) per the one-stylesheet rule.
// Marker and aria IDs are prefixed dg-writes-* — four diagrams share this page.
---

<figure class="post-figure post-diagram">
  <svg
    viewBox="0 0 700 502"
    role="img"
    aria-labelledby="dg-writes-title dg-writes-desc"
    class="dg"
  >
    <title id="dg-writes-title">
      What one change of address costs in Italy, Portugal and the United States
    </title>
    <desc id="dg-writes-desc">
      Three bands, each showing a person moving house and what has to be written.
      In Italy, one arrow leaves "you move" and reaches a single register, the
      ANPR, which then fans out automatically to five agencies: Agenzia delle
      Entrate, INPS, INAIL, ISTAT and the Motorizzazione. Written once, and five copies
      are fed from it. In Portugal, three arrows leave "you move" and reach three
      separate owners — Finanças for tax, the câmara for residence, AIMA for
      immigration — which all file against the same NIF join key, but the box
      that would hold a shared canonical record is drawn dashed and empty,
      because none exists. Three separate updates. In the United States, five arrows leave
      "you move" and reach the DMV, the IRS, the Social Security Administration,
      a bank and a utility company, with nothing in between them and nothing
      propagating. Five separate updates, and the copies drift apart.
    </desc>

    <defs>
      <marker
        id="dg-writes-arrow"
        viewBox="0 0 10 10"
        refX="9"
        refY="5"
        markerWidth="6"
        markerHeight="6"
        orient="auto-start-reverse"
      >
        <path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 z" class="dg-arrowhead" />
      </marker>
    </defs>

    {/* ================= ITALY — write once ================= */}
    <text x="6" y="22" class="dg-call dg-call--start">Italy — write once, and the copies are fed from it</text>

    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="6" y="68" width="104" height="48" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="58" y="97" class="dg-label">you move</text>
    </g>

    <g class="dg-edges" fill="none" marker-end="url(#dg-writes-arrow)">
      <path class="dg-edge dg-edge--key" d="M110 92 H 136" />
    </g>

    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="140" y="68" width="150" height="48" class="dg-node dg-node--anchor" />
      <text x="215" y="90" class="dg-label">ANPR</text>
      <text x="215" y="107" class="dg-sub">one authoritative update</text>
    </g>

    <g class="dg-edges" fill="none" marker-end="url(#dg-writes-arrow)">
      <path class="dg-edge" d="M290 92 H 320 V 40 H 352" />
      <path class="dg-edge" d="M290 92 H 320 V 66 H 352" />
      <path class="dg-edge" d="M290 92 H 352" />
      <path class="dg-edge" d="M290 92 H 320 V 118 H 352" />
      <path class="dg-edge" d="M290 92 H 320 V 144 H 352" />
    </g>

    <text x="362" y="44" class="dg-row">Agenzia delle Entrate</text>
    <text x="362" y="70" class="dg-row">INPS</text>
    <text x="362" y="96" class="dg-row">INAIL</text>
    <text x="362" y="122" class="dg-row">ISTAT</text>
    <text x="362" y="148" class="dg-row">Motorizzazione</text>

    <path class="dg-edge" d="M6 170 H 694" />

    {/* ================= PORTUGAL — one key, three writes ================= */}
    <text x="6" y="190" class="dg-call dg-call--start">Portugal — one key, but you write to every owner</text>

    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="6" y="236" width="104" height="48" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="58" y="265" class="dg-label">you move</text>
    </g>

    <g class="dg-edges" fill="none" marker-end="url(#dg-writes-arrow)">
      <path class="dg-edge" d="M110 260 H 150 V 224 H 188" />
      <path class="dg-edge" d="M110 260 H 188" />
      <path class="dg-edge" d="M110 260 H 150 V 296 H 188" />
    </g>

    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="192" y="208" width="196" height="32" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="290" y="229" class="dg-label">Finanças — tax</text>
    </g>
    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="192" y="244" width="196" height="32" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="290" y="265" class="dg-label">câmara — residence</text>
    </g>
    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="192" y="280" width="196" height="32" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="290" y="301" class="dg-label">AIMA — immigration</text>
    </g>

    <g class="dg-edges" fill="none">
      <path class="dg-edge dg-edge--key" d="M388 224 H 416" />
      <path class="dg-edge dg-edge--key" d="M388 260 H 416" />
      <path class="dg-edge dg-edge--key" d="M388 296 H 416" />
    </g>

    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="416" y="208" width="104" height="104" class="dg-node dg-node--anchor" />
      <text x="468" y="254" class="dg-label">NIF</text>
      <text x="468" y="273" class="dg-sub">one join key</text>
    </g>

    <path class="dg-node dg-node--out" d="M520 260 H 552" />

    <g class="dg-group dg-group--out">
      <rect x="552" y="208" width="142" height="104" class="dg-node dg-node--out" />
      <text x="623" y="252" class="dg-note">no shared canonical</text>
      <text x="623" y="268" class="dg-note">record — so the address</text>
      <text x="623" y="284" class="dg-note">is written three times</text>
    </g>

    <path class="dg-edge" d="M6 338 H 694" />

    {/* ================= US CITIZEN — no key, five writes ================= */}
    <text x="6" y="358" class="dg-call dg-call--start">US citizen — no key, and nothing between the copies</text>

    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="6" y="404" width="104" height="48" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="58" y="433" class="dg-label">you move</text>
    </g>

    <g class="dg-edges" fill="none" marker-end="url(#dg-writes-arrow)">
      <path class="dg-edge" d="M110 428 H 150 V 376 H 188" />
      <path class="dg-edge" d="M110 428 H 150 V 402 H 188" />
      <path class="dg-edge" d="M110 428 H 188" />
      <path class="dg-edge" d="M110 428 H 150 V 454 H 188" />
      <path class="dg-edge" d="M110 428 H 150 V 480 H 188" />
    </g>

    <text x="198" y="380" class="dg-row">DMV</text>
    <text x="198" y="406" class="dg-row">IRS</text>
    <text x="198" y="432" class="dg-row">Social Security Administration</text>
    <text x="198" y="458" class="dg-row">your bank</text>
    <text x="198" y="484" class="dg-row">your utility company</text>

    <text x="248" y="380" class="dg-note dg-note--start">— the New York address that never got updated</text>

    <text x="430" y="416" class="dg-call dg-call--start">five separate updates</text>
    <text x="430" y="436" class="dg-note dg-note--start">nothing propagates between them,</text>
    <text x="430" y="452" class="dg-note dg-note--start">so the copies drift — an update anomaly</text>
  </svg>

  <figcaption>
    Every band is the same move — you move house on the left, the records that
    need to know on the right — so the only thing that changes is what sits in
    between. Italy has a register. Portugal has a join key pointing at a record
    that doesn't exist; Finanças, the câmara and AIMA are three of its owners,
    not all of them. The US has nothing in the middle at all.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
